2186184919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1061 17047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbfd42f1-c449-5a1c-89f3-22f0da76f2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1061 17077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfb120ef-6495-557c-acdc-22fecb070cee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1064 "A matrix is said to be **nilpotent** if some positive power of the matrix is the zero matrix. For example, the matrix\n\n$$B = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis nilpotent because its second power is the zero matrix:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =O\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo state the definition more precisely: a matrix with the property $B^k=O$ for a number $k>0$ is known as a **nilpotent** matrix, and the lowest number $k$ such that $B^k=O$ is called the **index.**\n\nTherefore, our matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1065 3130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "39a61f3b-3f47-5962-a20c-33867d6f1127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1064 "A matrix is said to be **idempotent**, when multiplied by itself, yields itself.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$B = [\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis idempotent because $B^2 = B$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot (-6)+(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =B\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1065 3131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3977ba53-51f9-53db-b487-753096fe77e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1067 "Idempotent Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263308 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "230f8658-c0ec-55d0-a480-24839035e5f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263718 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 54 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263718 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263718 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "537a84f3-e5d6-5919-8f59-fa68e396bdea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264115 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 54 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264115 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264115 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81a8fd32-3158-5f3a-96c4-bee35c88b641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264723 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264723 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264723 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c48c76a8-87b3-54a5-9957-b4fab1c3eea9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265033 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e9c6aead-5e9f-5906-9990-0dcf405dc1cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186265033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265328 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2df157aa-3ba5-56ae-b49c-932b270763ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265944 #edb/ref 1071 "If $K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}]$, then ${K}^{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73bc7e3c-0d0e-5c12-afc8-020d832fd510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265944 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing ${K}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${K}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $K$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${K}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & ={K}^{2}\\cdot {K}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
